PART 3 — STANDARDS & IMPORT CERTIFICATION BARRIERS
3A. Mandatory DTI-BPS (PS/ICC) certification — CONCLUSION: does NOT apply to air terminals
• DTI-BPS maintains a "List of Products Under Mandatory Certification" : 82 PNS covering ~87–111 products (WTO G/LIC/N/3/PHL/17, 19/08/2025), divided into 3 groups: Electrical/Electronic; Mechanical/Building & Construction; Chemical/Consumer.
• Air terminal devices (grille, diffuser, register), dampers (including fire/smoke), louvers, VAV boxes, and sheet-metal duct are NOT on the mandatory list → EXEMPT . The only related product on the list is "Air conditioners" (complete household air-conditioning units, item #25). No air-conditioning components or parts are listed.
• The Mechanical/Building & Construction group lists only: BI/GI steel pipes, welded lightweight steel tubes, deformed/rerolled/angle steel bars, low-carbon steel wire, steel wire nails, plastics (PVC/PE/PB pipe), ceramic sanitary ware, ceramic tiles, portland/blended cement, plywood, CMU/AAC. No GI sheet, ductwork, dampers, diffusers, grilles, or louvers.
• Source: bps.dti.gov.ph (List of Products Under Mandatory Certification, 111 items); WTO G/LIC/N/3/PHL/17; DTI MC 20-49.
• Implication : STARDUCT does NOT need to obtain a PS license or ICC clearance for its core air-terminal products. This is a major advantage, lowering entry barriers/costs.
3B. Import duties & taxes — ATIGA 0% with Form D
Product | AHTN 2022 Code | MFN Duty | ATIGA (Vietnam, Form D)
Parts of air-conditioning machines (grille/diffuser/register classified as AC parts; fan-coil) | 8415.90.19 / 8415.90.99 | 10% | 0%*
Manually/automatically operated valves/dampers | 8481.80.xx (e.g. 8481.80.63) | 5% | 0%*
Parts of valves/dampers | 8481.90.90 | ~5% | 0%*
Iron/steel structures & structural parts (ductwork/support) | 7308.90.99 | 10% | 0%*
Other articles of iron/steel (sheet-metal fittings) | 7326.90.xx | 10–15% | 0%*
Aluminum ventilation grilles (if not classified as an AC part) | 7616 | 10–15% | 0%*
• *ATIGA = 0% for goods of Vietnamese origin, subject to a valid Form D (Certificate of Origin) plus compliance with the ATIGA Rules of Origin (specifically: minimum 40% Regional Value Content (RVC), or HS Code/CTC change — per the ATIGA Product Specific Rules, Vietnam National Trade Repository/moit.gov.vn). Per the ASEAN Main Portal (2025), "ASEAN is virtually tariff-free, with tariffs on around 98.86% of products fully eliminated under the ATIGA"; the upgraded ATIGA (10/2025) aims to eliminate 99% of tariff lines (ASEAN for Business Bulletin, 2026).
• 12% VAT applies to CIF value on EVERY shipment, even when the import duty is 0% (VAT-registered businesses can credit input VAT).
• Form D must be obtained by the EXPORTER in Vietnam BEFORE the goods leave port — it cannot be supplemented after the goods arrive in Manila. This is a common mistake that causes importers to pay the full MFN duty.
• HS classification is "fact-specific" : grilles/diffusers are only classified under 8415.90 when they are a "part of an AC machine"; if classified as a generic base-metal ventilation grille → 7326/7616 (higher MFN). Regardless of classification, Vietnamese goods remain at 0% under ATIGA — so the price advantage is preserved. An Advance Ruling from the Tariff Commission is recommended for borderline items (fire dampers, VAV boxes).
• Source: BOC AOCG Memos (2025-21, 2026-06, 24-2016); Philippine Tariff Finder (finder.tariffcommission.gov.ph); ATIGA/trade.gov.
3C. Fire safety — Fire Code RA 9514 & UL 555/555S
• RA 9514 (Fire Code of the Philippines) and its 2019 IRR require fire dampers in central air-conditioning ducts and smoke dampers/combination fire & smoke dampers at fire-rated partitions. The IRR clearly defines Fire Damper, Smoke Damper, and Combination Fire & Smoke Damper.
• RA 9514 does NOT explicitly name "UL 555" or "UL 555S" — the law only requires equipment that "automatically closes to prevent fire/smoke [spread]," and requires the Engineer/Architect-of-Record plus a Fire Safety Practitioner to certify design compliance with RA 9514. HOWEVER, specification convention (imposed by MEP consultants and international project owners, especially for data centers/airports/LEED projects) typically requires UL 555 (fire damper) and UL 555S (smoke damper) as the de-facto standard. The Bureau of Fire Protection (BFP) and building officials accept UL-labeled products, but review in practice relies on drawings/certification from the Engineer/Architect-of-Record plus a Fire Safety Practitioner.
• Key implication for STARDUCT : fire/smoke dampers do NOT require PS/ICC certification, but winning major projects REQUIRES UL 555/555S listing (or an accepted equivalent). This is a necessary certification investment for competing in the premium segment — confidence: high (as convention), but enforcement intensity varies between private/international projects (strict) and mainstream projects (lax).
3D. Standards used in practice
• The Philippine Society of Mechanical Engineers (PSME) Code , the Philippine Green Building Code (mandatory for buildings above a certain scale, directly referencing ASHRAE/AHRI), and PNS form the legal framework.
• ASHRAE / SMACNA / AMCA : widely referenced in specs for major projects (not a mandatory national law, but a de-facto technical standard applied by consultants). AMCA certification (particularly AMCA 500-D for dampers, AMCA 500-L for louvers) is a strong competitive advantage.
• BERDE (PhilGBC) is the national voluntary green building system (DOE-recognized, since 2009); LEED is common on premium/multinational projects. Both assess indoor air quality/ventilation → indirectly favoring air distribution products with certified performance data. Not mandatory, but increasingly influential on product selection.
3E. Practical market-entry requirements
• A domestic entity/importer (importer of record) is needed : STARDUCT is a supplier, NOT a contractor → does NOT need a PCAB license (PCAB, under RA 4566, applies only to contractors/construction firms, including MEP contractors; material suppliers are not covered). This distinction should be clarified: selling materials ≠ performing construction work.
• To sell into PH projects, STARDUCT should: (1) appoint a domestic agent/distributor (following the TROX-Dico Marketing model) to act as importer of record + warehousing + technical service; or (2) establish a company/JV in PH.
• PhilGEPS registration (philgeps.gov.ph) is required to participate in government tenders (typically through a local contractor/agent rather than a foreign supplier bidding directly).
• Form D is handled on the Vietnam side; the importer handles 12% VAT, BOC customs procedures, and an Advance Ruling if needed.
RECOMMENDATIONS (concrete, phased steps)
Phase 1 (0–3 months) — Foundations & Certification
1. Invest in AMCA certification (AMCA 500-D/500-L for dampers/louvers, performance data for grilles/diffusers) — this is the "ticket to entry" for being listed by consultants. Without it, STARDUCT cannot compete with Airmaster/Western brands.
2. Obtain UL 555/555S for the fire/smoke damper line — a practical requirement for data centers/airports/international projects.
3. Standardize the Form D process with Vietnamese customs for HS codes 8415.90, 8481.80, 7308/7326; obtain an Advance Ruling to lock in classification for fire/smoke dampers.
Phase 2 (3–9 months) — Distribution Channel & Relationships
1. Recruit 1–2 domestic agents/distributors to serve as importer of record (prioritizing those with existing relationships with MEP contractors such as Shinryo, MC Engineering, Kalayaan). Reference model: TROX-Dico Marketing.
2. Approach key MEP consultants directly (DCCD, RBSanchez, Guevara and Partners) to be added to the "approved manufacturers list" in specs — this is the decisive lever.
3. Register with PhilGEPS through the agent to access government projects (UP-PGH, airports, metro).
Phase 3 (9–18 months) — Project Penetration
1. Target priorities in order: (a) PEZA factories/industrial warehouses (price-sensitive, fewer barriers, high project volume — 314 projects worth ₱260.89 billion in 2025) → quick wins; (b) data centers (supporting air distribution + fire/smoke damper segment); (c) hospitals/cleanrooms (UP-PGH, PEZA pharma) once AMCA/UL certification is in hand.
2. Compete head-on with Airmaster on price plus the ATIGA 0% advantage and lead time.
Strategy-adjustment thresholds/benchmarks:
• If AMCA/UL certification is NOT obtained → target only the mainstream industrial/warehouse segment, competing on price against domestic fabricators (lower margins).
• If ATIGA/Form D runs into HS classification issues → seek an Advance Ruling immediately; if classified under 7326/7616 (higher MFN) instead of 8415.90, the price advantage is preserved either way since ATIGA is 0% for both.
• If a domestic agent reaches its target annual sales threshold → consider setting up a subsidiary/JV in PH to control the channel.
CAVEATS (data gaps & low-confidence areas)
1. The specific MEP/mechanical contractor for EACH data center/airport/metro project: COULD NOT be verified from public sources. Only the MMSP civil contractor (Shimizu–Fujita–Takenaka–EEI) and Narra''s EPCM (ENDEC) are confirmed. This is a priority to investigate through a domestic agent.
2. PH agents for Ruskin/Titus/Greenheck/Price/Systemair/Halton : specific names/contacts could not be verified; only TROX (Dico Marketing) and Airmaster (direct sales) are confirmed.
3. Segment-level market share (2C) : based on inference from regional convention, NOT on official PH market-share data.
4. UL 555/555S enforcement : a specification convention rather than an explicit RA 9514 legal requirement; enforcement intensity varies by project.
5. Pricing tier of domestic fabricators: no public price list available for quantitative comparison.
6. F.R. Sevilla, First Flow, Frostlite, Aleja, PBM pricing : contact information/details incomplete.
7. ATIGA 0% for each 8-digit HS code : confirmed via general ATIGA rules plus the FTA block in BOC memos, but not screenshot-verified line-by-line on the Tariff Finder for each 8-digit code (the site blocks automated access) — should be self-verified at finder.tariffcommission.gov.ph. With ~99% of ASEAN tariff lines at 0%, the 0% rate is nearly certain given a valid Form D.
8. Some projects (Ayala Land''s Laurean Residences, 67 stories, Makati) were suspended in 4/2026 due to rising construction costs — the pipeline carries volatility risk; always check the latest status on ppp.gov.ph and in the news before committing sales resources.
9. 1D — Commercial real estate : could not identify the specific MEP/M&E contractors typically used by SM Prime/Ayala Land/Megaworld/Robinsons Land/Filinvest, nor the HVAC/air-terminal brands specified in these conglomerates'' tender documents — requires direct investigation through bid records or industry relationships.', 'Aire Focus Corporation; Greentech Indl Inc', 'EPH', null)
