Gap analysis — positioning for STARDUCT
Indonesia''s market has a distinct "barbell" structure: at the bottom are numerous small-to-mid-sized fabrication shops (ASTEM, Indoducting, Adimas/POLAR, Surabaya Isolasitama, Satek Aire) competing on price/speed, uncertified — including for fire dampers (a genuine fire-safety risk). At the top are international brands with their own legal entity (Halton) or an official authorized agent (Systemair/Makswel), serving the premium/green segment designed by Meinhardt/Arup/WSP. The gap in the middle — mid-tier projects (mid-market commercial, hospitals, factories, government facilities) increasingly need reference to international standards for insurance/tender reasons but cannot afford TROX/Price/Greenheck pricing — is the clearest opening for STARDUCT: AMCA-aligned positioning, pricing more competitive than premium brands, with the performance data that domestic fabricators lack. The biggest risk is NOT product competition but TKDN (see Part 3B), which can completely block the government/BUMN project channel absent a domestic partner.
PART 3 — STANDARDS & IMPORT CERTIFICATION BARRIERS
3A. Mandatory SNI certification for finished products — does NOT apply to air terminals, but DOES apply to the RAW MATERIAL (a key distinction)
• Finished air terminal products (grille/diffuser/damper/louver): NO evidence found of a mandatory SNI applying specifically to these — the public list of mandatory SNI standards (BSN) is concentrated in bottled water, canned goods, household electrical appliances, textiles, etc. Complete air-conditioning units are regulated under MEPS/energy labeling ("Label Hemat Energi") per Permen ESDM 57/2017 — consistent with practice in the Philippines/Malaysia.
• WARNING — mandatory SNI for coated sheet steel RAW MATERIAL is about to tighten : Indonesia has long had mandatory SNI for galvanized coated sheet steel (BjLS, SNI 07-2053-2006), and is set to expand this significantly via Permenperin 23/2025 (effective 20/5/2026) — covering 7 product groups including BjLS, BjLSW, BjLAS/Galvalume (aluminum-zinc alloy coated steel — the most common ductwork material) , BjLASW, and hot/cold-rolled steel. Applies to IMPORTS AS WELL. The GINSI association reports (early 2026): no SPPT-SNI certificates have yet been issued despite the approaching deadline, cost is "tens of thousands of USD per product" plus the cost of sending inspectors to the country of origin, and the certificate is valid for only 1 year (versus the international standard of 3 years).
• Exemption mechanism (Permenperin 35/2019) : importers holding an API-P (producer-importer) license are automatically exempt; those holding an API-U (general importer) license need a technical confirmation letter from the Directorate General of Agro/Metal Industries. It is not yet determined whether finished ductwork/air terminal products (as opposed to raw steel coil) fall within or outside the scope of the new mandatory SNI, or how the exemption mechanism would apply — this is an important question to clarify before committing resources.
3B. TKDN (Local Content Requirement) — Indonesia''s most distinctive and serious barrier
This is the single biggest difference between Indonesia and the Philippines/Malaysia — neither of those two countries has a comparable localization requirement.
• Legal basis: Perpres 12/2021 (amending Perpres 16/2018) on public procurement; Law 3/2014 on Industry.
• TKDN threshold ≥25% : government agencies, BUMN, and BUMD are required to prioritize domestic products where a product meeting this threshold exists.
• TKDN + BMP (Bobot Manfaat Perusahaan / Company Benefit Weighting) threshold ≥40% : imported/foreign products are COMPLETELY BLOCKED from that tender — a hard barrier, not merely a price preference.
• The specific TKDN threshold for the HVAC/mechanical equipment category on the LKPP e-katalog: not yet determined — requires direct lookup on the e-katalog.lkpp.go.id tool (the "popularcommoditylist?jenis=TKDN" section) for the exact product category.
• A viable path for foreign exporters : import components/semi-finished goods (CKD) and complete assembly/processing/final inspection within Indonesia so they count toward TKDN; or gradually develop a domestic supplier. For STARDUCT, a viable strategy is exporting semi-finished goods/raw coil to a domestic partner for final processing/assembly rather than exporting finished products, if it wants to access APBN/APBD/BUMN projects.
• Good news : TKDN does NOT apply to private-sector projects (those not using state funds) — it only constrains APBN/APBD/BUMN projects and the e-katalog list. Many of the projects in Part 1 (private data centers, private hospitals such as Mayapada/Siloam/Hermina, private KEK factories) are NOT directly governed by TKDN — this is the priority initial market for STARDUCT.
3C. Import duties & taxes
• MFN/ATIGA duty by specific HS code (8415.90, 8481.80/90, 7308.90, 7326.90, 7616): not yet determined — requires direct lookup via the BTKI/INSW tool (beacukai.go.id), as the interactive database could not be accessed during this research session. In principle, ATIGA (Form D/self-certification) for Vietnamese goods applies a 0% rate to most standard product lines, but this needs line-by-line confirmation.
• Anti-dumping (BMAD) & safeguard (BMTP) duties on steel — an important hidden risk : Indonesia currently has BMAD in effect on hot-rolled steel coil (HRC, effective from 15/1/2025, PMK 103/2024, 5 years) against China/India/Russia/Kazakhstan/Belarus/Taiwan/Thailand — Vietnam is NOT on the list subject to duties in 2025 (however, an older BMAD regulation, PMK 224/2014, once listed Vietnam — it is not yet determined whether that regulation remains in force or has been superseded by the 2025 regulation). There is also a safeguard duty (BMTP) on aluminum-zinc coated steel (Galvalume, width >600mm, thickness <0.7mm) — in effect since 2014, extended multiple times — this is precisely the most common ductwork material; the current 2025-2026 duty rate/status and whether ASEAN-origin goods (Vietnam) are exempt under de-minimis rules are not yet determined .
• PPN (VAT) : 12% from 1/1/2025 (PMK 131/2024); for non-luxury goods, calculated on 11/12 of the import value (keeping the effective burden equivalent to the previous 11% rate).
• PPh Pasal 22 (income tax withheld at import) : 2.5% of import value for importers holding an API; 7.5% for those without an API (PMK 41/2022).
3D. Fire safety — Fire/smoke dampers
• Legal framework: Permen PU 26/PRT/M/2008 on technical requirements for building fire-protection systems; SNI 03-7012-2004 on smoke management for malls/atriums/large spaces.
• NO dedicated SNI product standard for fire/smoke dampers was found (no SNI equivalent to UL 555/555S). Market/consultant practice appears to reference UL 555/555S or NFPA (following the broader Southeast Asian pattern), but it could not be verified that any official PUPR document specifically cites UL 555.
• Implication for STARDUCT : closer to the Philippines than to Malaysia — there is no law naming a specific standard, and UL 555/555S + AMCA certification remains a widely accepted "best practice" without needing to invest in an additional local standard (unlike Malaysia''s MS 555/Bomba requirement).
3E. Practical market-entry requirements
• NIB (Nomor Induk Berusaha) : mandatory business identification number issued through the OSS system; when issued together with API-U/API-P, the NIB also serves as the import license number.
• API-U (import of finished goods for distribution/resale) vs API-P (producer import of raw materials/machinery for own production) — a company may hold only one type.
• Two lawful entry paths for foreign exporters : (1) establish a PT PMA (foreign-owned company) via OSS to hold its own API/NIB; or (2) appoint a domestic agent/partner as importer of record — without establishing a separate legal entity.
• Selling into government/BUMN projects : requires registering as a "penyedia" (provider) on LKPP''s SIKaP system and/or listing on the national e-Katalog . LKPP Perlkpp 1/2022 sets out procurement procedures for international goods/services — there is a separate process for foreign-origin goods, but the details require further verification. In practice, because TKDN so heavily dominates the process, foreign exporters without a TKDN-compliant domestic partner will be excluded from the e-katalog/state-project channel — reinforcing the recommendation to establish a joint venture/domestic processing partner from the outset if targeting the public sector.
RECOMMENDATIONS (concrete, phased steps)
Phase 1 (0–3 months) — Foundation, risk verification
1. Verify directly via BTKI/INSW (beacukai.go.id) the MFN/ATIGA duty for each relevant HS code — do not assume a blanket 0% rate.
2. Urgently verify the scope of Permenperin 23/2025 (mandatory steel SNI, effective 20/5/2026) — whether finished ductwork/air terminal products made of Galvalume/BjLAS would be treated as a "steel product" subject to mandatory SNI, or only raw coil. This is the largest cost/timeline risk in the entire report — it must be clarified before committing resources to large-volume exports.
3. Verify the TKDN threshold applicable to the HVAC/mechanical equipment category on e-katalog.lkpp.go.id — to decide whether a "export finished product" or "export semi-finished goods to a domestic processing partner" strategy is more appropriate.
4. Maintain the existing AMCA/UL 555-555S certifications (carried over from the Philippines/Malaysia strategy) — these remain a valid competitive advantage in Indonesia, with no need to invest in a separate local fire-damper standard (unlike Malaysia).
Phase 2 (3–9 months) — Distribution channel, TKDN strategy
1. Prioritize the PRIVATE sector first (Batam/GIIC data centers, private hospitals Mayapada/Siloam/Hermina, private KEK factories) — these projects are NOT governed by TKDN, allowing STARDUCT to sell directly through an agent/distributor without a complex joint-venture structure.
2. In parallel, evaluate a domestic partner for a long-term TKDN strategy if targeting government/BUMN projects (Kemenkes hospitals, LRT/MRT, IKN) — consider a model of exporting semi-finished goods plus final processing/assembly in Indonesia (via a partner such as ASTEM, Indoducting, or a newly formed joint venture) to count toward TKDN.
3. Engage MEP consultants (Meinhardt Indonesia, Arup Indonesia, WSP Indonesia, Arkonin) — position as an AMCA-aligned "spec risk reduction" solution for green/LEED/Greenship projects without the premium-brand price tag (Halton/Systemair).
Phase 3 (9–18 months) — Project penetration
1. Priority targets: (a) private data centers in Batam/GIIC-Bekasi (largest pipeline, no TKDN, already has a real MEP case study — Binkei/BW Digital); (b) private hospitals (Mayapada, Siloam, Hermina — many projects under construction, no TKDN); (c) private KEK factories (Kendal, Batang, Gresik — Rp0.5-10 trillion scale per project, suited to the "conventional building" segment); (d) government/BUMN/IKN projects ONLY AFTER a TKDN-compliant partner is in place.
2. Closely monitor developments around Permenperin 23/2025 and the response from GINSI/importer associations — if the regulation is delayed/relaxed (as GINSI is currently lobbying for), the steel-certification cost risk could decrease.
Strategy-shift thresholds/benchmarks
• If Permenperin 23/2025 is confirmed to apply to BOTH finished ductwork products and raw coil → consider shifting entirely to an "export semi-finished goods + process in Indonesia" model rather than exporting complete products, to both avoid finished-product SNI and accumulate TKDN.
• If the TKDN threshold for the HVAC category is low (easily achievable through local assembly/final inspection) → a domestic joint venture is worth pursuing early to unlock the entire government/BUMN market (very large — IKN, Kemenkes hospitals, LRT/MRT).
• If the scope of TKDN/steel SNI CANNOT be verified within the first 3 months → give absolute priority to the private sector and defer plans to approach the public sector until clearer data is available.
CAVEATS (data gaps & low-confidence areas)
1. The specific MEP/mechanical contractor for EACH project : confirmed for only a small number of projects (Binkei — Batam data center; Hutama Karya — IKN airport; Waskita/Jakpro — LRT; Indotek/CRCC — Bali LRT; PTPP — Makassar hospital). Most hyperscalers/private owners keep this information confidential.
2. The specific TKDN threshold for the HVAC/mechanical equipment category on the LKPP e-katalog : the exact percentage could not be found — this is the highest-priority risk, with a direct impact on the ability to sell into the public sector, and should be verified first.
3. The scope of Permenperin 23/2025 for finished ductwork/air terminal products (versus raw steel coil only): not yet determined — the second-largest cost/timeline risk.
4. Precise MFN/ATIGA duty rates and BMAD/BMTP status by 8-digit HS code : could not be looked up via INSW/BTKI during this research session — particularly important to confirm the current status of the Galvalume safeguard duty (BMTP) and whether Vietnam is exempt under ATIGA/de-minimis rules.
5. Indonesian agents for TROX/Titus/Krueger/Price Industries/Greenheck/Metalaire : could not be verified — this may represent a genuine market gap (opportunity) or simply a search limitation.
6. The default specification standard used by major MEP consultants (pure SNI vs. ASHRAE/AMCA layered on top): not directly verified via tender documentation — only indirect evidence from green/LEED projects.
7. Conflicting national data center capacity figures between government sources (~500MW in 2025) and private market reports (~1.44GW) — the measurement methodology should be clarified before using this to estimate opportunity size.
8. Several projects carry notable schedule risk: Soekarno-Hatta Terminal 4 (fully cancelled 11/2024 — a lesson in always checking the latest news), North Bali Airport (multiple sources skeptical of the timeline), the Jakarta-Surabaya HSR line (still at the negotiation stage, no confirmed budget), and the Trans-Sumatra railway (still at the capital-structuring stage, no mass construction yet).
9. Pricing/price tier of domestic fabricators (ASTEM, Indoducting, Adimas, etc.): no public price list available for quantitative comparison — a recurring limitation from the earlier Philippines/Malaysia reports.', 'PT. Sinabu Nusaindo', 'EID', null)
