Gap analysis — where STARDUCT fits
Thailand has a fairly clear two-tier structure: at the bottom are Bangkok/Pathum Thani/Samut Prakan fabricators (Duct Asia, Airmax, King Air Grille, Komfortflow) competing on price/speed, uncertified — including fire dampers. At the top are international brands operating through 2 models: certified local manufacturing (Ruskin — the most serious threat) or premium import agencies (TROX via Approach Engineering, Halton via its own subsidiary) serving institutional/flagship projects (hospitals, MRT, government) designed by Arup/Meinhardt/EEC Engineering Network. The gap in the middle is where STARDUCT fits: mid-tier commercial/industrial projects (EV plants/EEC, hotels, mid-tier hospitals) specified by consultants at the level of EEC Engineering Network, increasingly requiring internationally standardized performance data (LEED/WELL) but with budgets that cannot stretch to Ruskin/TROX pricing. The most viable near-term entry point is private commercial/industrial projects in the EEC executed by EPCs such as Italthai Engineering, rather than flagship government/MRT projects where agency relationships (Approach-TROX) are already deeply entrenched.
PART 3 — STANDARDS & IMPORT CERTIFICATION BARRIERS
3A. Mandatory TIS (มอก.) certification — does NOT apply to finished air-terminal products, but DOES apply to raw galvanized steel material
• Finished air-terminal products (grille/diffuser/damper/louver/duct): no mandatory มอก. standard specifically found — the TISI portal (JavaScript-based, cannot be crawled directly) needs to be manually checked to fully confirm this, but indirect evidence suggests the mandatory มอก. list is concentrated on household electrical appliances, toys, and structural steel building materials — with no "HVAC air-terminal equipment" category.
• Complete air-conditioning units : regulated under มอก./energy MEPS (Ministry of Energy/EGAT) — consistent with the practice seen in the 3 countries surveyed so far.
• Galvanized sheet steel DOES have a mandatory มอก. standard : TIS 50-2561 (มอก. 50-2561) — "เหล็กแผ่นเคลือบสังกะสี" (zinc-coated sheet steel) — mandatory, requiring an import notification plus TISI product certification. However, the scope applies ONLY to raw/semi-finished steel in coil, strip, or cut-sheet form — it does NOT apply to fully fabricated ductwork/grille products (a structure similar to Indonesia''s but WITHOUT the same sudden tightening seen in Indonesia''s Permenperin 23/2025). This needs to be verified directly with TISI regarding how finished ductwork goods are classified at the border.
3B. Made in Thailand (MiT) — a "semi-hard" localization mechanism
• Legal basis: the Ministerial Regulation on Procurement of Preferred Domestic Goods/Services (Version 2), B.E. 2563 (2020), issued under the Public Procurement and Supplies Management Act B.E. 2560 (2017). Certification authority: the Federation of Thai Industries (FTI) via the mit.fti.or.th portal.
• Domestic-content threshold to obtain MiT status : an "ASEAN Content"-style model — a minimum of ~40% Thai domestic value-added/materials. Importantly, this is open to foreign-invested enterprises , provided they hold a valid business license/tax registration in Thailand (i.e., have actual local manufacturing/assembly) — 100% Thai ownership is not required despite what the name might suggest.
• Public procurement mechanism : public procurement must reach a minimum of 60% of total value in domestic goods; construction works involving steel specifically must reach ≥90% MiT-certified . This is a near-mandatory threshold (not just a scoring preference), with an exemption mechanism requiring higher-authority approval. Not fully verified is the exact scope (whether it applies to all government works or only "megaprojects"), since the source (Bangkok Post) was inaccessible (paywalled).
• Private-sector projects are NOT bound by MiT — the law applies only to state-budget/state-enterprise spending.
• Implications for STARDUCT : lighter than Indonesia''s TKDN (no confirmed absolute "hard-block" threshold, and the 40% localization bar is much lower than Indonesia''s combined TKDN+BMP≥40% which stacks additional factors) but still a substantive barrier for steel construction works (potentially including steel ductwork) — a local assembly/final-fabrication joint venture in Thailand should be considered if targeting government/megaproject work.
3C. Import duties, anti-dumping duties & VAT
• MFN/ATIGA duty by specific HS code (8415.90, 8481.80/90, 7308.90, 7326.90, 7616): unverified — needs to be checked directly with the Thai Customs Department (customs.go.th) or the ASEAN Tariff Finder. Thailand''s average non-agricultural MFN duty is ~7% (2024); ATIGA (Form D/self-certification) from Vietnam is in principle close to 0% for most industrial goods, but this has not been confirmed line by line.
• SERIOUS WARNING — Anti-dumping (AD) duty specifically targeting Vietnamese steel : Thailand is applying an AD duty (extended for a further 5 years following a sunset review) on hot-dip galvanized cold-rolled steel and pre-painted steel (PPGI/PPGL) originating from Vietnam , covering 39 HS codes , with rates of 4.30%–60.26% depending on the exporter/manufacturer. This is a typical ductwork/air-terminal material. Separately, the AD duty on cold-rolled aluminum-zinc-coated steel (GL, 32 HS codes) from Vietnam ended on 12/5/2023 . The list of 39 HS codes subject to the PPGI/PPGL duty MUST be reviewed immediately to confirm whether it overlaps with STARDUCT''s input materials or export HS codes — this is the most serious cost risk identified across all 4 Southeast Asia reports produced so far, because it targets Vietnamese origin directly rather than being a general barrier.
• VAT : the standard statutory rate is 10%, but a reduced rate of 7% is currently in effect — the Cabinet has extended the 7% rate through 30/9/2026 . This applies to imports, calculated on CIF value plus import duty.
3D. Fire safety — fire/smoke dampers
• Legal framework: the Building Control Act B.E. 2522 (1979) and related ministerial regulations; the Cabinet approved a package tightening fire-safety requirements for 2023-2024 for high-rise/super-tall buildings (≥2-hour structural fire resistance for high-rises) — but NO provision specific to ductwork/fire dampers was found in the accessible summaries.
• There is a มอก. 2541 (TIS 2541) fire-safety standard, but at a general system/building level; it is unverified whether a specific sub-clause exists for fire/smoke dampers as a certified product in their own right (equivalent to UL 555/555S).
• Actual market practice : according to a Thai HVAC industry source (parameth.info), the Thai market relies heavily on international standards UL 555 (fire damper), UL 555C (ceiling damper), UL 555S (smoke damper), NFPA 90A/80/105 rather than purely on TIS standards — products are expected to carry a "UL Certified/Listed" mark, particularly for premium/high-rise projects.
• Implications for STARDUCT : closer to the Philippines/Indonesia than to Malaysia — no law names a specific mandatory standard, and existing UL 555/555S + AMCA certification remains a widely accepted "passport" without needing further investment in a separate local standard.
3E. Practical market-entry requirements — the Foreign Business Act is a significant barrier
• Foreign Business Act (FBA) B.E. 2542 : trading/import-distribution activity falls under the restricted category (List 3) — majority foreign-owned firms wishing to conduct direct import/distribution activity need either: (1) a Foreign Business License (FBL) — a discretionary approval process (~1-3 months), with uncertain outcome and conditions on Thai/foreign staffing ratios and minimum capital; or (2) BOI promotion — but the BOI generally does NOT apply to pure trading/distribution activity, only to actual manufacturing/assembly ; or (3) partnering with a majority-Thai-owned agent/distributor .
• Market reality : having a reputable local representative/agent is considered "practically mandatory" to win government tenders (per trade.gov); Thailand is NOT a member of the WTO Government Procurement Agreement (GPA), so there is no commitment to non-discrimination toward foreign suppliers.
• e-GP registration (the electronic government procurement system) is mandatory to bid on government tenders — it is unverified whether a foreign entity not yet registered in Thailand can register directly, though the evidence strongly suggests a Thailand-registered entity is required.
• Practical conclusion — the "dual benefit" of setting up local manufacturing/assembly : if STARDUCT establishes an assembly/fabrication facility in Thailand (encouraged by the BOI, avoiding FBA restrictions), that same facility also helps meet the 40% localization threshold needed for MiT certification (Section 3B) — unlocking both the private-sale channel and the public-procurement channel simultaneously. This is a positive point of difference from Indonesia (where TKDN and import licensing are two separate issues).
RECOMMENDATIONS (concrete, phased next steps)
Phase 1 (0–3 months) — Foundations, AD-duty risk review
1. Urgently review the 39 HS codes subject to the PPGI/PPGL anti-dumping duty on Vietnamese steel (rate 4.30%-60.26%) — cross-check against STARDUCT''s input-material and planned export-product HS codes. This is priority #1, as an overlap could neutralize the entire cost advantage.
2. Verify MFN/ATIGA duty rates directly with the Thai Customs Department/ASEAN Tariff Finder for the remaining HS codes (8415.90, 8481.80/90, 7326.90, 7616).
3. Verify the scope of TIS 50-2561 with TISI or a certification consultancy (TÜV SÜD, Blue Asia Labs) — confirm that finished ductwork products are not classified as "galvanized sheet steel" subject to the mandatory มอก. standard.
4. Maintain existing AMCA/UL 555-555S certifications — these remain a valid advantage, with no need to invest in a separate local fire-damper standard.
Phase 2 (3–9 months) — Legal structure, distribution channel
1. Evaluate a model for setting up a small assembly/fabrication facility in Thailand (encouraged by the BOI for manufacturing, avoiding FBA barriers for pure trading, while also accumulating the 40% localization threshold for MiT) — this Thailand-specific "dual benefit" should be prioritized over simply appointing an agent.
2. If not yet ready to invest in manufacturing, recruit a majority-Thai-owned agent/distributor to act as importer of record — avoiding FBA entanglement.
3. Approach EEC Engineering Network and international consultants (Meinhardt/Arup/WSP Thailand) — position as an AMCA-aligned, spec-risk-reducing solution for green-building projects (TREES/LEED/WELL) without Ruskin/TROX pricing.
4. Carefully assess Ruskin (Thailand) — the most serious competitor, with local manufacturing plus full certification — a clear pricing/service positioning strategy is needed to compete, rather than relying solely on being "cheaper than imports" as in the Philippines.
Phase 3 (9–18 months) — Project penetration
1. Priority targets: (a) private EV/industrial plants in the EEC (BYD, Changan, Sunwoda and similar — not directly bound by MiT since they are private projects) executed by EPCs such as Italthai Engineering; (b) private hospitals (BDMS/Samitivej/Bumrungrad — multiple projects under construction); (c) private data centers (EEC/Chonburi — enormous pipeline, no MiT exposure); (d) government/megaprojects (airports, MRT) ONLY AFTER an MiT-compliant structure is in place.
2. Monitor the schedule risk of the 3-airport high-speed rail link closely (real risk of collapse) — this should not be treated as a certain near-term opportunity despite being an EEC flagship project.
Strategic decision thresholds
• If overlap with the PPGI/PPGL AD-duty HS codes is confirmed → consider alternative steel sourcing (not Vietnamese cold-rolled galvanized steel) or shift toward exporting more deeply fabricated products to change HS classification (requires an Advance Ruling from Thai Customs).
• If the 40% MiT threshold is readily achievable through local assembly/final inspection → prioritize early small-scale manufacturing investment in Thailand to unlock both the public and private markets.
• If Ruskin (Thailand) dominates the premium fire/smoke damper segment too strongly → focus STARDUCT first on the mainstream/mid-tier grille/diffuser/louver segment, reserving fire dampers for a later stage once a foothold is established.
CAVEATS (data gaps & low-confidence areas)
