MEP contractors active in the Cambodian market (not yet linked to specific projects above): TEAMS, Daiku MEP Solution, ET&S (ETS Engineering), TKT Engineer (previously did MEP work for the Cambodian National Assembly building) — these are more viable access points than selling directly to developers.
Assessment: Per CBRE, the Phnom Penh condo market added only ~3,900 units in the first half of 2025 and is under oversupply pressure. The opportunity for STARDUCT is not high volume but rather tracking a handful of landmark projects by AEON, Chip Mong, and OCIC — where the grille/diffuser/damper volume per project can be substantial even though the number of projects is small. Because most major developers (Japan''s AEON, China''s Prince/CRUCG) typically bring their own familiar general and MEP contractors, STARDUCT should approach through domestic/regional MEP contractors (TEAMS, Daiku, TKT) as the entry point. Confidence note : no specific MEP contractor could be identified for any project — the largest data gap, with a direct effect on sales-channel access; no air-terminal brand/spec currently in use was found; the current (2026) status of Chip Mong 271 Mega Mall and Prince Square has not been confirmed as up to date (source data is from 2020-2021).
PART 2 — AIR TERMINAL SUPPLIER COMPETITIVE MAP
2A. Domestic Cambodian Manufacturers
Key finding : NO certified domestic Cambodian air-terminal (grille/diffuser/damper) manufacturer — or even a clear domestic product brand — was found, which is markedly different from the previous 4 countries (where there was always at least one layer of domestic fabrication competing on price).
Company | Actual Role | Notes
Fast Cambodia | Ductwork fabrication (round/spiral/rectangular duct, flexible duct, fittings, GI sheet metal, fire-rated ducting) | The closest thing to a "domestic manufacturer," but only at the raw ductwork level — does NOT list grilles/diffusers/dampers/air terminals as a distinct product line
ET&S Engineering , Daiku MEP Solution , GGEAR | Domestic MEP/HVAC installation contractors (not product manufacturers) | Reference projects: Techo Airport, Sihanoukville Port, Calmette Hospital, Sofitel Phnom Penh, Aeon Mall — these are genuine target customers , not competitors
Conclusion: domestic ductwork shops/MEP contractors likely self-fabricate simple, uncertified grilles/registers during installation (a reasonable inference, though not directly confirmed by a source) — there is no genuine "branded competitor" for STARDUCT to face head-on.
2B. International Brands & Their Presence in Cambodia
Brand | Presence in Cambodia | Notes
Duct Asia Engineering (Thailand) | NO dedicated facility in Cambodia — serves cross-border from Thailand, claims to have exported/undertaken contracts in Cambodia/Myanmar/Laos since 2006 | A textbook example of the "serve the region from a neighboring country" model — exactly as predicted at the outset
Systemair (Sweden) | Served via Systemair Malaysia — confirmed case study: supplied AXC axial fans and AJ/AJR jet fans for the 36-story GDT Office Building, Phnom Penh | Confirms Cambodia is served from the Malaysia regional office, with no dedicated legal entity
Daikin (Japan) | Has established its own sales company in Cambodia (12/2023) — the first major HVAC brand to formally establish a direct presence | Mainly air conditioners/VRF, not air terminals — but a signal that the market is gradually formalizing
TROX, Ruskin/Titus/Krueger/Greenheck, Price Industries, Halton, Airmaster Equipment Emirates | NO agent, reference project, or presence of any kind was found in Cambodia , despite direct searches by brand name + "Cambodia" | A genuinely notable market gap — unclear whether it is because the market is too small for these brands to bother with, or because it is an untapped opportunity
2C. MEP Consultants & Contractors
• Meinhardt (Cambodia) — a dedicated office in Phnom Penh (50+ professionals, part of Meinhardt Group Singapore), reference projects: Foreign Trade Bank Tower, Novotel Phnom Penh, Canadia Bank data center. The website does not specify which reference standards it uses.
• Arup (Cambodia) — a confirmed legal entity in Phnom Penh, involved in green transport studies, interested in consulting on the Phnom Penh MRT system.
• Foster + Partners — architect for Techo International Airport; the specific MEP contractor/consultant is undetermined.
• China-funded project (the Tboung Khmum Friendship Hospital, built by China Railway Construction Group) — likely to use Chinese GB design standards rather than ASHRAE/AMCA (a reasonable inference, not directly confirmed).
• Key conclusion : NO Cambodia-specific national ventilation/HVAC design standard was found — private/international projects (hotels, private hospitals) likely default to ASHRAE via international consultants (Meinhardt/Arup), while state projects funded by China may use GB standards. This is a "dual-standard" environment not encountered in the previous 4 countries.
Gap analysis — positioning for STARDUCT
Cambodia does not have the "barbell" competitive structure (cheap domestic vs. premium brand) seen in the previous 4 countries — simply because there is almost no genuine air-terminal competitor present on the ground . This admits two readings, and both should be presented honestly to STARDUCT rather than picking one: (1) The opportunity reading — a genuine gap for an AMCA-aligned, mid-tier-priced supplier with geographic proximity (land border, shorter logistics than Thailand/Malaysia/China) to become the default choice for Cambodian MEP contractors (ET&S, Daiku, GGEAR) and international consultants (Meinhardt/Arup), who currently must make do with uncertified domestic products or slower/more expensive imports from Thailand/Malaysia/China. (2) The cautious reading — this very "emptiness" (no major brand has felt the need to set up its own entity, and the non-residential MEP contractor base is still small) may suggest that genuine total demand is small and concentrated in a handful of commercial/hotel/healthcare/SEZ factory projects in Phnom Penh/Siem Reap/Sihanoukville — possibly enough to support only an agent/distribution-partner model, not a large direct investment. Given STARDUCT''s Vietnam base, the most viable entry channel is partnering with one of the Phnom Penh MEP contractors (ET&S/Daiku/GGEAR) as both a distribution channel and a spec-influence channel, while China-funded super-infrastructure projects (airports, canals, large hospitals) are likely a closed channel, dominated by Chinese-standard supply chains.
PART 3 — STANDARDS & IMPORT-CERTIFICATION BARRIERS
3A. CS (Cambodian Standard) Certification — the lightest of the 5 countries
3B. Import Duties & Taxes
• VAT : a flat 10% on CIF value plus import duty — clearly confirmed and stable.
• MFN import duty : an ad valorem tariff schedule ranging 0%-35% depending on HS code — the specific rates for the relevant codes (8415.90, 8481.80/90, 7308.90, 7326.90, 7616) are unverified ; direct lookup on the ASYCUDA/GDCE database is required (inaccessible during this research session due to robots.txt blocking).
• ATIGA (Form D) : Cambodia is an ASEAN member and applies preferential ATIGA tariffs (trending toward 0% for most intra-bloc goods) to Vietnamese goods with a valid Form D (Cambodia is also rolling out an ASEAN-wide self-certification mechanism) — specific rates by HS code are unverified.
• Special Tax : typically targets vehicles, alcohol, tobacco, fuel, and certain electronics — no indication it applies to structural steel/aluminum/HVAC fittings , though this has not been 100% confirmed against the current GDCE tariff schedule. The tax/rate adjustment effective 1/1/2026 (DFDL) focuses on electronics/EVs/household appliances — no mention of HVAC/ductwork/damper HS codes , suggesting no special change (positive or negative) for STARDUCT''s product category.
3C. QIP (Qualified Investment Project) — no localization barrier
3D. Fire Safety
• Cambodia DOES have a national fire code: the National Fire Safety Systems Code , effective from 12/8/2019 (13 chapters, 7 annexes), applying to new-build/renovation projects (excluding explosives/fireworks warehouses), and defining high-rise thresholds (27m for residential/24m for other types).
• Unverified whether this code contains specific technical provisions for fire dampers/smoke dampers (equivalent to UL 555/555S), or whether it stops at the level of fire alarm/evacuation/firefighting — the full text could not be accessed during this research session.
• Following the general Southeast Asian pattern, and given the absence of a Cambodia-specific mechanical/HVAC standard, foreign/international hotel/private hospital projects are likely to default to referencing UL 555/555S or NFPA 90A/92 via foreign architects/consultants — this is a reasonable inference based on the regional pattern, not directly confirmed for Cambodia.
• Implication for STARDUCT : existing UL 555/555S + AMCA certifications are likely to remain acceptable, with no need to invest in a separate local standard — but this should be verified directly through a pilot project or an on-the-ground consultant before being treated as certain.
3E. Practical Market-Entry Requirements — the easiest of the 5 countries
RECOMMENDATIONS (concrete, phased steps)
Phase 1 (0-3 months) — Confirm the real opportunity size, without assumptions
1. Verify directly with ISC (isc.gov.kh) the current mandatory CS list — confirm 100% that no HVAC air-terminal category exists (the referenced ASEAN document is from 2012 and needs updating).
2. Verify specific MFN/ATIGA duty rates via GDCE/ASYCUDA for the relevant HS codes.
3. Assess the real scale of demand through direct contact with the 2-3 identified Phnom Penh MEP contractors (ET&S Engineering, Daiku MEP Solution, GGEAR) — they are the best real-world source of information on annual air-terminal purchase volumes, something public search cannot provide.
4. Maintain existing AMCA/UL 555-555S certifications — no need for additional investment for this market at this early stage.
Phase 2 (3-9 months) — Establish channels, leverage the simple procedures
1. Given that 100% foreign ownership is permitted, consider setting up a small representative office/subsidiary directly in Phnom Penh (rather than relying solely on an agent) — the procedure is far simpler than in Thailand/Indonesia, and it can take advantage of QIP if light assembly/fabrication is planned.
2. Establish a partnership/distributor relationship with 1-2 identified domestic MEP contractors (ET&S/Daiku/GGEAR) — they serve as both customers and a spec-influence channel for private developers.
3. Approach Meinhardt (Cambodia) and Arup (Cambodia) — the two international consultants with an on-the-ground legal entity — to understand exactly which spec standards they actually use (likely ASHRAE) and get STARDUCT onto their approved-supplier lists.
Phase 3 (9-18 months) — Selective market entry
1. Priorities: (a) private/public hospitals with cleanroom scope (Calmette''s 3 new buildings, UHS Teaching Hospital, the $300 million 8-conglomerate hospital) — high value per project, well-suited to STARDUCT''s AMCA strengths; (b) international hotels/commercial projects designed by Meinhardt/Arup; (c) SEZ factories with a significant office/administrative-area scope (Minebea, new electronics plants) rather than heavy-process factories (tires).
2. DO NOT prioritize China-funded super-infrastructure projects (the next phase of Techo Airport, the Funan Techo Canal, Chinese friendship hospitals) in the early stage — the supply channel is likely already locked in by Chinese supply chains.
Strategy-shift thresholds/benchmarks
• If direct contact with ET&S/Daiku/GGEAR shows annual purchase volumes are too small → keep Cambodia at a light agent/distribution level, do not invest in a dedicated office, and reallocate resources to Indonesia/Thailand.
• If it is confirmed that Meinhardt/Arup Cambodia consistently use ASHRAE/AMCA for private projects → accelerate investment in the consultant relationship, which would be the most cost-effective channel of the 5 countries surveyed.
• If CS certification scope is found to be expanding in the future (Cambodia''s institutions are still developing) → monitor periodically, since the current "no-barrier" status could change as the ISC matures.
CAVEATS (data gaps & low-confidence areas)
1. The specific MEP/mechanical contractor for EACH project : could not be verified at all for any project across the whole of Part 1 — the highest level of confidentiality/lack of disclosure among the 5 countries surveyed. This needs to be investigated through direct contact rather than public search.
2. The official number of SEZs and total CDC/CSEZB investment capital : varies widely between sources (25-57 zones) — no unified official figure exists, and the CDC website shows placeholder data when accessed.
3. The technical content of the 2019 National Fire Safety Systems Code relating to fire/smoke dampers: the full text could not be accessed — only a high-level summary is available.
4. Precise MFN/ATIGA import duty rates by HS code : could not be looked up via GDCE/ASYCUDA (automated access blocked).
5. The current mandatory CS list from ISC : the main reference source (the ASEAN compilation) is dated (2012) — needs direct verification against the latest version on isc.gov.kh.
6. International air-terminal competitors that may serve Cambodia "anonymously" via regional agents (Thailand/Vietnam/China) without leaving an online footprint — the possibility of undetected competition via search cannot be ruled out.
7. Some project values are not independently verified (e.g. the Wanli Tire plant at $500 million, Minebea land at $650 million) — sourced from a single reference only, requiring further cross-checking.
8. Overall market size remains the biggest open question : this research confirms the existence of specific projects but CANNOT estimate Cambodia''s total annual air-terminal demand through public-search methods — it is recommended that this be treated as a mandatory next step before committing significant resources.', 'TNR Industries Co., Ltd', 'ECA', null)
