('SA', 'Saudi Arabia', '• Vietnam has NO FTA with Saudi Arabia/the GCC — the ASEAN-GCC FTA negotiations are still at an early stage (negotiating round launched 5/2025, not yet signed). STARDUCT is subject to the full MFN/CET tariff: the GCC baseline rate is 5%, but since 6/2020 Saudi Arabia has unilaterally raised tariffs on numerous base-metal lines (steel, aluminum — directly relevant to HS 7308/7326/7616) to 10-20%. VAT of 15% is levied on CIF+duty — the highest among the markets studied so far.
• SABER is the largest procedural barrier : since 1/1/2021, EVERY import shipment — including "non-regulated/low-risk" products such as standard grilles/diffusers — must obtain a Product Certificate of Conformity (PCoC) plus a Shipment Certificate of Conformity (SCoC) per shipment before customs clearance is granted. This is a fundamental difference from ASEAN/Hong Kong/Singapore, where low-risk goods are typically exempt from mandatory electronic certification.
• Good news on the technical side : the Saudi Building Code (SBC 501 Mechanical, SBC 801 Fire) was developed in cooperation with the ICC (US), localized from the International Mechanical Code — meaning ASHRAE/SMACNA/AMCA are highly likely to be the default standards in MEP specifications, unlike markets leaning toward UK/EU standards. This is a structural advantage for STARDUCT''s AMCA-aligned positioning. HOWEVER, localization pressure (LCGPA, Saudi Aramco''s IKTVA) creates a bid-scoring disadvantage for purely imported goods on state-owned projects.', 'MARKET INTELLIGENCE REPORT: Saudi Arabia Construction & HVAC Sector — Market-Entry Assessment for STARDUCT (Vietnam)
Prepared: 14/08/2026. Audience: CEO / Business Development. Style: direct, data-driven, explicitly flagging data gaps. This report runs parallel to the previously prepared Philippines/Malaysia/Indonesia/Thailand/Cambodia/Hong Kong/Macau/Singapore/South Korea/UAE reports — same methodology, same analytical framework.
Important note before reading : Saudi Arabia presents two barriers not seen in previously researched markets: (1) SABER — mandatory electronic certification registration for ALL imported goods (no "low-risk goods exempt from certification" carve-out as in many other countries), and (2) high "hype/descope" risk on the giga-projects (NEOM "The Line" has been sharply scaled back, New Murabba/Mukaab construction has been paused) — it is essential to distinguish projects genuinely under construction from those that remain communications targets. At the same time, this is also the market with the highest VAT (15%) and a high likelihood that ASHRAE/SMACNA/AMCA are the default standards (favorable for STARDUCT), since the Saudi Building Code is modeled on the US code.
TL;DR
• Vietnam has NO FTA with Saudi Arabia/the GCC — the ASEAN-GCC FTA negotiations are still at an early stage (negotiating round launched 5/2025, not yet signed). STARDUCT is subject to the full MFN/CET tariff: the GCC baseline rate is 5%, but since 6/2020 Saudi Arabia has unilaterally raised tariffs on numerous base-metal lines (steel, aluminum — directly relevant to HS 7308/7326/7616) to 10-20%. VAT of 15% is levied on CIF+duty — the highest among the markets studied so far.
• SABER is the largest procedural barrier : since 1/1/2021, EVERY import shipment — including "non-regulated/low-risk" products such as standard grilles/diffusers — must obtain a Product Certificate of Conformity (PCoC) plus a Shipment Certificate of Conformity (SCoC) per shipment before customs clearance is granted. This is a fundamental difference from ASEAN/Hong Kong/Singapore, where low-risk goods are typically exempt from mandatory electronic certification.
• Good news on the technical side : the Saudi Building Code (SBC 501 Mechanical, SBC 801 Fire) was developed in cooperation with the ICC (US), localized from the International Mechanical Code — meaning ASHRAE/SMACNA/AMCA are highly likely to be the default standards in MEP specifications, unlike markets leaning toward UK/EU standards. This is a structural advantage for STARDUCT''s AMCA-aligned positioning. HOWEVER, localization pressure (LCGPA, Saudi Aramco''s IKTVA) creates a bid-scoring disadvantage for purely imported goods on state-owned projects.
