Gap analysis: the market has a clear two-tier structure — (1) ~15-17 local manufacturers already AMCA-certified (SAFID, Sharqawi, Dania, Ventilation Industries, ATAI...) across Riyadh/Jeddah/Dammam/Al-Jubail, competing on price, lead time, and localization scoring; (2) a small group of global brands (Systemair having established a Riyadh regional HQ, TROX/Halton maintaining representative offices) serving the high-spec segment. Zamil — despite being the largest name in the Saudi HVAC industry — operates at the packaged-equipment tier and is not a direct competitor at the air-terminal tier. STARDUCT''s realistic opportunity sits in the middle ground: an AMCA-aligned manufacturer at mid-range pricing, serving projects where consultants (especially US-origin/international firms) specify ASHRAE/SMACNA/AMCA standards — a technical language STARDUCT is already fluent in — but where budget does not stretch to Tier-1 global brands. The biggest barrier: localization pressure (LCGPA) — since 2022, state-owned enterprise tenders (Aramco, SABIC, Ma''aden...) apply a minimum 30% local-content weighting in bid scoring, with a price margin of up to 10% favoring domestic SME suppliers — purely imported products from Vietnam will face a bid-scoring disadvantage despite competitive pricing on state-owned-investor projects. The most viable strategy: (a) target private/sector projects not subject to LCGPA constraints, (b) find a final-assembly/fabrication partner in KSA to reach the minimum localization threshold, (c) become a sub-supplier to local AMCA-listed manufacturers who lack capacity in certain niche product lines.
PART 3 — STANDARDS & IMPORT CERTIFICATION BARRIERS
3A. Mandatory Product Certification — SASO/SABER (SALEEM)
• Saudi Arabia operates the SALEEM program through the SABER electronic platform (saber.sa), managed by SASO, integrated with the FASAH customs clearance system.
• Critically important point : since 1/1/2021 , SABER registration has been mandatory for ALL imported goods , not just "regulated" products. Every shipment requires: a Product Certificate of Conformity (PCoC, valid for 1 year per model/HS code) and a Shipment Certificate of Conformity (SCoC, issued per shipment, fee ~SAR400, must be cleared within 60 days). No SCoC = no customs clearance — a major difference from ASEAN/Hong Kong/Singapore, where "low risk" goods are typically not subject to 100% mandatory electronic certification.
• SABER splits products into 2 groups by HS code: Regulated (High/Medium Risk) — requires conformity assessment by a SASO-accredited body (R-CAB), which may include sample testing/factory audits; Non-regulated (Low Risk) — self-declaration only, no mandatory third-party testing, though the per-shipment SCoC fee still applies.
• The "Technical Regulation for Building Materials – Part 1 (Metals and Alloys)" has been checked: its scope covers only rebar, steel plate, structural steel, and structural aluminum — it does NOT include ductwork, dampers, grilles, or diffusers.
• Conclusion (medium confidence) : passive manual grilles/diffusers/dampers and steel/aluminum duct accessories → likely fall under the non-regulated/low-risk group → still require SABER registration + PCoC (self-declared) + per-shipment SCoC, but no mandatory lab testing/factory audit. Dampers with an integrated electric actuator/motor → potentially fall under the Gulf Technical Regulation for Low Voltage Electrical Equipment → placing them in the regulated group requiring third-party certification.
• Mandatory recommendation : STARDUCT must look up each actual HS code on saber.sa (account required) to confirm the exact regulated/non-regulated classification — an unavoidable due-diligence step before quoting.
3B. Import Duties & VAT
• Saudi Arabia applies the GCC Common External Tariff (CET): a standard minimum rate of 5% for most industrial goods from outside the GCC.
• Important : since 10/6/2020, Saudi Customs has unilaterally raised import duties on more than 2,000 tariff lines across 57 HS chapters (beyond WTO bound-rate commitments), with base metals (iron, steel, aluminum) among the affected groups , at new rates of 10%, 15%, and even 20% — significantly above the standard 5% CET.
• WTO data (2022): Saudi Arabia''s average non-agricultural MFN tariff is 5.6%; specifically, "minerals & metals" averages 8.0%, and "other manufactures" averages 10.6% — indicating HS codes 7308/7326/7616 (iron/steel and aluminum products) are likely subject to rates above 5%.
• Vietnam has no FTA with Saudi Arabia/the GCC. The ASEAN-GCC FTA negotiations remain at an early stage (proposed 10/2023, negotiating round launched 5/2025), not yet signed, not yet notified to the WTO — STARDUCT is subject to the full MFN/CET tariff.
• VAT: 15% (raised from 5% to 15% effective 1/7/2020), levied on CIF + import duty, collected via ZATCA at the point of customs clearance — the highest among the markets studied so far.
Product | HS Code | MFN/GCC CET Duty | VAT
Air conditioning/ventilation components (valves, mechanical dampers) | 8481.80 | ~5% standard CET; the 12-digit code needs verification as it may fall in the group raised in 2020 (10-20%) | 15%
Air conditioning machine parts | 8415.90 | ~5% (no evidence found of inclusion in the raised-tariff group) | 15%
Iron/steel structures (frames, ductwork) | 7308 | Potentially 10-20% due to the 6/2020 metals tariff increase — not precisely confirmed | 15%
Other articles of iron/steel (steel grilles, diffusers) | 7326 | Potentially 10-20% — not precisely confirmed | 15%
Other articles of aluminum (aluminum grilles, diffusers) | 7616 | Potentially 10-20% — not precisely confirmed | 15%
A direct lookup of the Saudi Customs Integrated Tariff (eservices.zatca.gov.sa) by 12-digit HS code is required for precise figures — the table above is indicative of risk only.
3C. Fire Protection Regulations — Saudi Civil Defense / Saudi Building Code
• The correct designation for Saudi Arabia''s fire code is SBC 801 (Saudi Fire Protection/Fire Code), overseen by the Saudi Building Code Committee and enforced by the Directorate General of Civil Defense (Saudi Civil Defense/DGCD).
• SBC 801 and related codes reference NFPA standards (consistent with the Saudi Building Code''s origin as a code modeled on the US system, developed in cooperation with the ICC).
• No direct textual citation has been found confirming that SBC 801 references UL 555/555S for fire/smoke dampers — this has not been confirmed at the specific clause level. Per common (independently unverified) industry practice, products bearing UL 555/555S listing are typically accepted by Civil Defense as evidence of compliance.
• Beyond SASO/SABER, fire/smoke dampers typically require a separate approval from Saudi Civil Defense (fire equipment registration, sometimes through a Civil Defense-recognized agent) — an additional layer of barrier independent of SABER, which STARDUCT should verify directly with a fire-protection consultant in Saudi Arabia before offering fire damper lines.
3D. Applicable Technical Standards
• The Saudi Mechanical Code (SBC 501) was developed in cooperation with the ICC — localized from the US International Mechanical Code (IMC), similar to how SBC 801 is based on NFPA.
• Due to this lineage, MEP consultants in Saudi Arabia (especially international/US firms) in practice default to referencing ASHRAE, SMACNA (duct construction), and AMCA (grille/diffuser/damper performance) — unlike markets influenced by British standards (BS/CIBSE). Good news for STARDUCT, as its AMCA-aligned positioning matches Saudi specification expectations directly.
• No specific clause in SBC 501 explicitly naming SMACNA/AMCA has been cited — further verification through the full SBC 501 text or actual MEP tender documentation is recommended.
3E. Practical Market-Entry Requirements
• SABER registration : a foreign manufacturer can register as a "Manufacturer" on SABER through an authorized representative in Saudi Arabia, or a Saudi-based importer/distributor (holding a CR) can handle the per-shipment PCoC/SCoC registration — in practice most exporters choose a local importer since a Saudi CR number is required.
• Local agent requirement : Saudi Arabia''s Commercial Agency Law has been reformed (effective expected mid-2023), opening the door to foreign investors acting as agents/distributors, and no longer mandating that exclusive agents be Saudi/GCC nationals — however, in practice most foreign HVAC manufacturers still maintain a local distributor/agent to handle SABER, customs clearance, Civil Defense approvals, and after-sales service.
• Nitaqat (Saudization) : applies only to legal entities registered in Saudi Arabia. If STARDUCT exports solely through an independent distributor, the Nitaqat burden falls on the distributor. If STARDUCT establishes its own legal entity in Saudi Arabia, it must comply with Saudi labor quotas based on company size/sector classification (Platinum–Red).
• IKTVA / LCGPA : IKTVA is Saudi Aramco''s internal program, applying only to Aramco''s direct suppliers, not a general legal requirement. For government procurement broadly, the wider framework is the LCGPA (Local Content and Government Procurement Authority) — which sets minimum local-content ratios and price-preference margins for highly localized products in public tenders. If STARDUCT wants to penetrate the giga-projects deeply, a partial local assembly/manufacturing model in Saudi Arabia (joint venture or satellite plant) would carry a clear competitive advantage over pure export.
RECOMMENDATIONS (specific, phased steps)
Phase 1 (0–6 months) — Address the SABER barrier first
1. Top priority : register a SABER account (via a local representative/importer) and look up the exact HS code for each STARDUCT product to confirm regulated/non-regulated classification — this is a prerequisite that cannot be skipped, unlike in other markets where this step can be deferred.
2. Confirm the exact import duty by 12-digit HS code via the Saudi Customs Integrated Tariff — in particular checking whether STARDUCT''s steel/aluminum products fall within the group affected by the 2020 tariff increase (10-20%) or remain at the standard 5% CET, since this gap significantly affects price competitiveness.
3. Review the project list — remove NEOM "The Line" and the not-yet-started portions of New Murabba from near-term sales planning; prioritize Diriyah Gate, Qiddiya Phase 1, King Salman Airport, and Riyadh Metro.
Phase 2 (6–12 months) — Distribution channel & positioning leveraging the AMCA advantage
1. Recruit 1 agent/importer in Saudi Arabia to hold the SABER registration — prioritizing a partner already connected with mid-tier MEP contractors (NAC Arabia, Arabian Bemco, MEP Vision) over the mega-conglomerates, which are harder to access.
2. Leverage the structural advantage: the ASHRAE/SMACNA/AMCA standards are the default in Saudi Arabia (unlike many UK/EU-leaning markets) — emphasize AMCA-aligned positioning when approaching international MEP consultants (Buro Happold, WSP, Dar Al-Handasah).
3. Assess the possibility of partnering with or becoming a sub-supplier to local AMCA-listed manufacturers (SAFID, Sharqawi, Dania) that lack capacity in certain niche product lines, rather than competing head-on.
Phase 3 (12–24 months) — Project penetration, avoiding the localization trap
1. Prioritize private-sector/segments not subject to LCGPA constraints (private hospitals BEC Arabia/National Medical Care, private real estate projects) before targeting state-owned giga-projects (Aramco, SABIC, ROSHN) — where bid scoring is disadvantaged by a lack of localization.
2. If order volumes are large enough, evaluate the feasibility of a final assembly/fabrication model in Saudi Arabia (via a joint venture with one of the local AMCA-listed manufacturers) to reach the minimum localization threshold for LCGPA/IKTVA, opening the way into the giga-projects.
Strategy-shift thresholds/benchmarks:
• If the cost/time required for SABER registration is too high relative to initial order volume → consider deferring Saudi Arabia and prioritizing markets with lower procedural barriers first.
• If steel/aluminum products fall into the 20% tariff group (rather than 5%) → re-assess price competitiveness against local AMCA-listed manufacturers (SAFID, Sharqawi, Dania) before committing further market investment.
CAVEATS (data gaps & low-confidence areas)
1. Exact classification of grilles/diffusers/dampers into the regulated/non-regulated group on SABER : unverified — STARDUCT must look this up directly on saber.sa by specific HS code.
2. Exact import duty by 12-digit HS code (specifically whether HS 7308/7326/7616 fall within the group raised to 10-20% in 2020): unverified — requires lookup on the Saudi Customs Integrated Tariff.
3. Specific citation of UL 555/555S within the SBC 801 text : unverified at the clause level — based only on inference from common industry practice.
4. The specific MEP/mechanical contractor for EACH project (data centers, infrastructure, hospitals, commercial real estate): CANNOT be verified from public sources for most projects.
5. Accuracy of the giga-project figures (NEOM 1GW/6GW data center, ROSHN 400,000 units): likely communications targets rather than signed construction commitments — should be continuously monitored via MEED/Arab News before being incorporated into sales planning.
6. The Saudi agent/distributor for Titus, Krueger, Price Industries, Greenheck, Ruskin : unverified; only Systemair, TROX, and Halton have confirmed presence.
7. Segment-level market share (2C) : based on inference from industry structure, no official market-share data available.
8. 1D — Commercial real estate : no specific MEP contractor or specified HVAC brand identified in the tender documentation for ROSHN/New Murabba/Qiddiya.', null, null, null)
