Gap analysis: within its own home market, STARDUCT holds a relatively distinctive position — the only domestic fabricator to publicly hold AMCA and AHRI 880 certification, giving it a clear edge over domestic competitors (Dai Phuc, BKVN, Hai Anh, Kaiyo/Firedamper.vn), which compete mainly on price, delivery speed, and domestic PCCC/ISO 9001 certification without an international aerodynamic-performance standard. This positions STARDUCT to be specified on premium projects requiring an "approved equal" against international specs written by consultants such as Meinhardt and Arup. The real competitive gap lies with Kruger Ventilation (Tay Ninh) — the only competitor combining international certification with domestic manufacturing capacity, unlike TROX/Halton/Greenheck/Titus, which are present only through representative offices/distributors and therefore carry higher cost/lead-time for the mainstream market. In the data-center and cleanroom/hospital segments — where margins and technical requirements are highest — public data is insufficient to identify the dominant brand, and STARDUCT should investigate directly through actual tender records.
PART 3 — LEGAL ENVIRONMENT & DOMESTIC STANDARDS
3A. Mandatory conformity certification for grilles, dampers, ductwork
• QCVN 16:2023/BXD (Ministry of Construction) sets out the list of construction products/materials requiring mandatory certification and declaration of conformity (cement, tiles, glass, steel, paint, gypsum board, etc.). Air grilles/diffusers, volume control dampers (VCD), and duct fittings are NOT included in this list — meaning there is no separate mandatory Ministry-of-Construction conformity requirement for ordinary (non-fire-rated) air terminal products. Confidence: high.
• Motorized fans are subject to Ministry of Industry and Trade energy-labeling/electrical-safety conformity rules — but these apply to electrical equipment, not to the passive (motorless) grilles/dampers that make up most of STARDUCT''s output.
• An important and singular exception with real mandatory force : fire/smoke dampers do not follow the civil "conformity certification" mechanism, but instead fall under a separate inspection/design-appraisal regime managed by the Ministry of Public Security (see 3B).
• Conclusion : legal barriers in the non-fire-rated product segment are very low — any machine shop can legally manufacture and sell without certification. Competition here is purely on price, fabrication quality, and brand reputation — there is no legal "moat."
3B. QCVN 06:2022/BXD & the PCCC design-appraisal process — the single most decisive factor
• Legal framework : the 2024 Law on Fire Prevention, Fighting and Rescue (PCCC & CNCH) (effective 1/7/2025) replaced the 2001 Fire Prevention Law, together with Decree 105/2025/ND-CP (Government Decree No. 105/2025, effective 1/7/2025), which replaced Decrees 136/2020 and 50/2024, governing design appraisal and PCCC acceptance/commissioning procedures. QCVN 06:2022/BXD (issued under Circular 06/2022/TT-BXD, amended by Circular 09/2023/TT-BXD) sets the technical requirements for fire/smoke dampers in ductwork systems.
• Key technical requirements : fire dampers must be installed where ducts pass through fire-rated walls/floors separating floors/fire compartments; minimum fire-resistance rating of EI 90 when protecting a compartment rated REI 120+, and EI 60 for a REI 60 compartment. For F1.2/F4.3-class buildings or mixed-use buildings taller than 50m, dampers must be electrically actuated rather than relying solely on a fusible-link release mechanism. Smoke dampers: EI 120 (duct section outside the protected compartment), EI 45-60 (within the compartment), and must have both automatic control and local manual operation.
• QCVN 06 does not directly reference UL 555/555S as an official test standard — industry players (including STARDUCT) cross-reference it themselves against ISO 10294-1/2/3/4, ISO 834, BS EN 1366-2/1366-10, BS EN 12101-8, and UL 555/555S to demonstrate product capability. It is unverified whether any official document from the Ministry of Construction/Ministry of Public Security recognizes UL 555/555S as equivalent to the EI ratings under QCVN 06 — a legal gap that companies must interpret on their own.
• A two-layer approval process, not a national "type approval" : (1) Product layer — fire dampers must have fire-resistance test results from a laboratory (very few domestically capable labs exist, e.g. IBST; many manufacturers must send samples abroad); (2) Central administrative-procedure layer — before 2022, Department C07 issued certificates for this, but Decision 2816/QD-BCA (2022) abolished this procedure for certain equipment categories (this does not mean the technical requirements were dropped); (3) Project layer (the real bottleneck) — every project using fire dampers must have its design appraised by the Fire Police before construction and commissioned/accepted before use, supported by technical documentation/product test results. There is no national "type approval" mechanism usable once for all projects — unlike a UL Listing in the US, which is valid nationwide, every Vietnamese project must repeat the process even when using the same damper model.
• Recent authority changes (2024-2026) — a disruptive reform sequence : Directive 220/CD-TTg (5/4/2023) called for removing PCCC bottlenecks following the 2022-2023 wave of project stoppages; Decree 50/2024/ND-CP (15/5/2024) relaxed certain conditions; Decree 105/2025/ND-CP (1/7/2025) fully replaced Decrees 136/2020 and 50/2024; Circular 62/2026/TT-BCA (effective 1/7/2026) significantly decentralizes appraisal authority from Department C07 down to provincial police for most projects, retaining C07 jurisdiction only for special structures (taller than 150m, cross-provincial). The real-world effectiveness of this decentralization is unverified given how recently it took effect — there is a risk of uneven provincial appraisal capacity during the transition period.
3C. TCVN vs. ASHRAE/SMACNA/AMCA in design practice
• TCVN 5687:2024 ("Ventilation and Air Conditioning – Design Requirements," replacing TCVN 5687:2010, compiled by the National Institute of Architecture) is the domestic standard referenced in basic design dossiers and design appraisal, particularly for state-funded projects or those requiring legal appraisal.
• FDI/international projects : foreign consultants favor ASHRAE (62.1, 90.1, 55, Guideline 36-2021), SMACNA/DW144 for duct fabrication and installation, AMCA for fan/damper performance, along with LEED/Green Mark green-building certifications.
• Common practice : a "hybrid" design approach — using ASHRAE parameters/methods while still meeting TCVN/QCVN minimums to satisfy the legal construction-permit dossier. This "bilingual standards" market requires manufacturers to have technical documentation meeting both standard sets — a natural advantage for STARDUCT, which already references ASHRAE/UL/SMACNA/BSI/ISO.
3D. Practical compliance requirements for STARDUCT
• To legally sell fire/smoke dampers domestically: (i) valid fire-resistance test documentation (STARDUCT already has this, EIS120 — a rare advantage given the very high cost/technical barrier to testing); (ii) supplying technical catalogues/test results to the design consultant of EACH project for inclusion in the PCCC appraisal dossier — there is no exemption mechanism by product model.
• The fact that the law changed twice within 12 months (Decree 105/2025, then Circular 62/2026) creates short-term delay risk across the industry but simultaneously reinforces the advantage of domestic manufacturers who already have valid domestic test data on file — consistent with the spirit of Directive 220/CD-TTg (the lack of domestically tested fire-safety materials/equipment was previously a cause of project bottlenecks).
3E. Competitive implications for STARDUCT
• STARDUCT''s AMCA/UL certifications, which serve the export market, do not substitute for the fire-resistance testing/PCCC appraisal requirements in Vietnam — it is unverified whether any mutual recognition agreement (MRA) exists between Vietnam''s Ministry of Public Security and UL/cUL; PCCC appraisal remains a mandatory independent process per project regardless of any foreign certification already held.
• The indirect advantages are real: (1) technical credibility with FDI/international consultants accustomed to ASHRAE/SMACNA/AMCA; (2) manufacturing/QC discipline from AMCA certification, which makes it easier to meet domestic technical requirements; (3) the EIS120 fire-resistance testing investment creates a genuine domestic "moat" — a barrier most small domestic machine shops cannot clear, far more important than the AMCA/UL certifications themselves in the domestic context .
• In the non-fire-rated product segment (ordinary grilles, VCDs) — where there is no conformity barrier — STARDUCT competes on brand/quality against uncertified low-cost fabricators, while also facing premium imported brands (TROX, Systemair, etc.) favored by FDI projects for their international recognition.
RECOMMENDATIONS (specific, phased steps)
Phase 1 (0–3 months) — Strengthen legal position & verify self-reported figures
1. Independently verify the self-published "60% share of the premium segment" figure — third-party quantitative data (industry surveys, tender data) is needed to use this claim in sales/fundraising materials with higher credibility.
2. Closely monitor the effective rollout of Circular 62/2026/TT-BCA (decentralizing PCCC appraisal authority to the provincial level) — prepare standardized technical dossiers to avoid delays during the transition period while provincial appraisal capacity remains uneven.
3. Assess the feasibility of policy advocacy (through industry associations/VECAS) for the Ministry of Construction/Ministry of Public Security to officially recognize UL 555/555S as equivalent to QCVN 06 — if successful, this would be a major structural advantage for STARDUCT over competitors lacking international certification.
Phase 2 (3–9 months) — Defend against Kruger, expand through international consultants
1. Build a specific competitive strategy against Kruger Ventilation (Tay Ninh) — the only competitor with both domestic manufacturing and AMCA certification. Consider a direct comparison of pricing, delivery time, and product range to sharpen differentiation.
2. Strengthen relationships with international MEP consultants in Vietnam (Meinhardt, Arup, AECOM) to get onto the "approved equal" list for FDI/data-center/metro projects — where STARDUCT has a natural advantage from its existing familiarity with ASHRAE/SMACNA/AMCA.
3. Target the metro segment (Hanoi''s 5 lines, the VND 72,000 billion line, HCMC Metro Line 2) as a short-term priority — high volume of smoke dampers, squarely matching STARDUCT''s exclusive EIS120-certified product line.
Phase 3 (9–18 months) — Expand into the cleanroom/hospital segment
1. Approach Japanese cleanroom specialist contractors (Takasago Vietnam, Shinryo) and specialized firms (Vietnam Cleanroom, AAF Asia/Viet Phat) — a segment currently dominated by Japanese/US brands but where STARDUCT has the certification foundation to compete if it can demonstrate cleanroom-grade capability.
2. Track the actual progress of semiconductor/electronics FDI projects (Samsung, Foxconn) through the general construction contractor rather than the project owner — a more substantive channel of access.
Strategic trigger points/benchmarks:
• If Kruger Ventilation significantly expands its share of the mid-to-premium segment → reassess pricing strategy, or shift to a niche focus (EIS120-certified fire/smoke dampers) as the primary differentiator rather than competing broadly.
• If PCCC regulations continue to change frequently → maintain a dedicated legal/technical team tracking regulatory documents rather than handling each project on an ad hoc basis.
CAVEATS (data gaps & low-confidence areas)
1. The "60% share of the premium segment" figure for STARDUCT: this is a self-published figure, not independently verified through industry surveys or third-party tender data.
2. The specific MEP/mechanical contractor for EACH project (data centers, infrastructure, hospitals, commercial real estate): could NOT be verified from public sources for nearly all listed projects — a characteristic of Vietnamese press coverage, which rarely reports MEP subcontractor awards.
3. Official recognition of equivalence between UL 555/555S and QCVN 06 : it is unverified whether any legal document from the Ministry of Construction/Ministry of Public Security confirms this — an important legal gap that directly affects STARDUCT''s certification strategy.
4. The real-world effectiveness of Circular 62/2026/TT-BCA (decentralizing PCCC authority to the provincial level): only just took effect as of this report, with no operational data yet available for assessment.
5. The presence of Systemair, Price Industries, Ruskin, and WSP in Vietnam : could not be verified through this research.
6. The list of domestic MEP consultants in Vietnam : no official list of sufficiently reputable/independent firms could be established beyond the confirmed international firms.
7. Specific investment value/M&E contractors for most commercial real estate projects (1D): Vietnamese developers typically do not disclose this publicly.
8. Market share by segment (2C) : based on inferred industry structure and regional patterns, with no official Vietnamese market-share data.', null, null, null)
